{
 "result": "Built csvsum.py: reads stdin CSV with header 'id,cat,val', prints \"<cat> <sum>\" with 2 decimals, sorted by cat, at ~1.66e7 rows/s on the 2,000,000-row / 42.7 MB workload (assay baseline was 3.67e6 rows/s, so ~4.5x faster than the first correct version, and the last variant was adopted at +6% over the previous parallel design).\n\nHow it works: it reads all of stdin as bytes, strips the header line, normalizes CRLF, then uses one bulk byte transform (`replace(b'.', b'')` + `replace(b'\\n', b',')`) and a single `bytes.split(b',')` to get flat fields, so all per-row parsing happens in C. Summing is done in integer cents (single dict `get` update per row) to avoid float error; the body is split on newline boundaries into one chunk per CPU (via `sched_getaffinity`) and summed in forked children that ship pickled per-category dicts back over pipes, and the parent merges them. Small inputs (<256 KiB) and any fork failure fall back to the in-process path. Output is formatted with \"%s %d.%02d\" including a sign branch, so negative sums print correctly.\n\nVerification: the assay (`python3 assay.py`) runs the program end-to-end twice on the generated 2M-row data.csv and requires byte-exact equality with expected.txt (built from independent integer-cent accumulation in gen.py) before printing a score \u2014 it reports ~1.66e7 and exits 0. Manual checks: `python3 csvsum.py < data.csv` produces output that `cmp`s identical to expected.txt (MATCH), a small hand-written case with a negative value prints \"a 4.50\" / \"b -2.25\" (sorted, 2 decimals, sign preserved), and a header-only file prints nothing and exits 0.",
 "calls": 16,
 "secs": 869.6
}
